<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

	<PropertyGroup>
		<!-- The two targets DotGram.Web has, for its reasons: netstandard2.0 is what
		     every consumer can read, and net10.0 is what the newest runtime gets without
		     the shims. -->
		<TargetFrameworks>netstandard2.0;net10.0</TargetFrameworks>

		<Description>A C#-style expression language written in .gram, compiled straight into System.Linq.Expressions trees.</Description>
		<PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
		<PackageTags>expressions;expression-tree;linq;linq-expressions;parser;csharp;dsl;dotgram</PackageTags>
		<PackageReleaseNotes>Since 0.2.0. The language reads more of C#'s initializers, with, target-typed new and nullable types, refuses what C# refuses in them, and parses faster.

BREAKING CHANGES, and what to do

- Initializers are read as C# reads them, so texts 0.2.0 accepted are refused: an entry of a member initializer that is not Name = … (CS0747); an assignment as an element of a collection initializer, { 1, x = 2 }, { b.Capacity = 2 }, { x += 1 } - 0.2.0 read x = … as an assignment to a variable and added it (write the assignment in parentheses, { (x = 1), 2 }, to keep it an element); a member set twice in one initializer (CS1912); an initializer of the members of a property of a value type (CS1918); a collection initializer on a type that is not IEnumerable (CS1922); an initializer that assigns through a setter the text cannot call or a property with none (CS0200) or a read-only field (CS0191); an object creation that leaves a required member unset (CS9035).
- A switch expression with no natural type as the operand of a plain operator (1 + (x switch { … }), !(x switch { … })) is refused, naming the two arms that meet in nothing (CS8506), where it reached the operator's factory with no type. String concatenation still takes it, boxed to object.
- A member's own initializer, Items = { 1, 2 }, reads the member once for each entry inside it, as C# does, where it read it once for all; the tree is then a block of assignments, not a MemberInit.
- The public type ExpressionParser.ParserInput&lt;T&gt; is removed; nothing public took or returned it.

NEW

- A trailing comma after the last entry of an object, collection or array initializer; empty braces as an object initializer that sets nothing (new List&lt;int&gt; { }, Items = { }); indexers among members (new Dictionary&lt;int, int&gt; { [1] = 2 }), built as a block of assignments in C#'s order.
- new[] { 1, 2L }, typed by the one type its elements convert to (CS0826 where there is none).
- x with { Name = value, … } over a record class or a struct (CS8858 for any other type); with stays a name a variable may have.
- new(…) with its type left out, wherever the place it stands in says the type: a declaration, a member or element of an initializer, an argument, a return, a cast, a throw, a ?: typed by its place.
- T? as System.Nullable&lt;T&gt; in every type position; string? is string; int?? is refused (CS8629). After is and as, a bare ? is read as nullable only where what follows could not begin an expression, as C# reads it.
- A switch with no natural type converts to object as to any other target.

FASTER

- The parser reads with the immediate carrier, building the tree as it reads: accepted text parses about 30 to 55% faster (deeply nested text the most) and the generated parser is about 9% smaller. A text refused late is about 30% slower and allocates more; one refused early costs what it did. The answers - tree, refusal, message, exception - are unchanged.
- Member initializers nested and never closed are read once at every level, where they were read twice (three quarters of a second at seventeen levels). A string left unclosed inside an interpolated string's hole, $"{x", is refused at once, where the time doubled with every further character.</PackageReleaseNotes>

		<!-- The documentation of what is public, shipped beside the assembly. Here and not
		     in Directory.Build.targets: by the time those are read the SDK has already
		     decided where the file goes, and it goes nowhere. -->
		<GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>

		<!-- Symbols inside the assembly, and paths normalized on CI only: both as
		     DotGram.Web has them, and the reasons are written there. -->
		<DebugType>embedded</DebugType>
		<ContinuousIntegrationBuild Condition="'$(GITHUB_ACTIONS)' == 'true'">true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>

		<!-- Generated files land on disk so a reader can open what the grammar became. -->
		<EmitCompilerGeneratedFiles>true</EmitCompilerGeneratedFiles>
		<CompilerGeneratedFilesOutputPath>$(BaseIntermediateOutputPath)GeneratedFiles</CompilerGeneratedFilesOutputPath>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<!-- The generator, and nothing else: the parser it writes acquires no runtime
		     dependency (docs/syntax.md §6.1). -->
		<ProjectReference Include="..\DotGram\DotGram.csproj"
		                  OutputItemType="Analyzer" ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup Condition="'$(DotGramAnalyzeGenerated)' == 'true'">
		<!-- The generator's own output, analysed for dead or redundant emitted code
		     (tools/DotGram.GeneratedAnalyzers), never packed, loaded only under this
		     property (docs/development.md). -->
		<ProjectReference Include="../../tools/DotGram.GeneratedAnalyzers/DotGram.GeneratedAnalyzers.csproj"
		                  OutputItemType="Analyzer" ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'netstandard2.0'">
		<!-- For the consumer: the generated parser takes `ReadOnlySpan<char>`. The one
		     dependency this package has, and only here. -->
		<PackageReference Include="System.Memory" />

		<!-- For the compilation: types the framework lacks, emitted internal, so nothing
		     reaches the package. -->
		<PackageReference Include="Meziantou.Polyfill" PrivateAssets="all" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<PropertyGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'netstandard2.0'">
		<MeziantouPolyfill_IncludedPolyfills>T:System.Runtime.CompilerServices.IsExternalInit;T:System.Index;T:System.Range;T:System.Diagnostics.CodeAnalysis</MeziantouPolyfill_IncludedPolyfills>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />

		<!-- The skill beside it, so that an agent working in a project that has restored
		     this package can read how to use it without leaving the machine. The README
		     says where it is, in a comment nothing renders. -->
		<None Include="SKILL.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<!-- What the tests and the hand-written yardstick read and no caller should: the
		     factories the grammar builds with, the resolver, a publication written
		     `internal`. It costs nothing across the boundary since the attributes the
		     generator writes into each assembly are marked embedded, and neither sees the
		     other's. -->
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="DotGram.Tests" />
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="DotGram.Tests.Slow" />
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="DotGram.Benchmarks" />
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="DotGram.Handwritten" />
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="DotGram.Trace.Tests" />
	</ItemGroup>

</Project>
